\c 2
\s1 Nevukadnetstsar's dream
\rem /s1 Daniel's wisdom; Nebuchadnezzar's Dream
\p
\v 1 \add Back in\add* the second year¦356721 of¦356724 King Nevukadnetstsar's¦356724 reign¦356723, \add *the king\add* had had some dreams¦356725 that troubled¦356728 him—leaving him unable to sleep¦356730.
\v 2 \add *He¦356740\add* summoned¦356736 the¦356737 magicians¦356737, fortune-tellers, sorcerers¦356739, and¦356740 \add ≈astrologers\add* to \add ≈explain\add* the dreams¦356743 to \add *him\add*, so they¦356744 came¦356744 in and¦356745 stood¦356745 in front¦356746 of¦356746 the¦356747 king¦356747.
\v 3 “I've¦356753 had¦356753 a dream¦356753,” the¦356751 king¦356751 told¦356749 them¦356750, “and¦356754 my¦356755 spirit¦356755 is¦356754 \add ≈anxious to¦356756 understand¦356756\add* \add *it¦356749\add*.”
\v 4 The¦356762 \add ≈astrologers\add* spoke¦356761 to¦356763 the¦356763 king¦356763 in Aramaic¦356764, “Long live¦356767 King¦356763 Nevukadnetstsar. Tell¦356768 us what you dreamt¦356769, and¦356761 then¦356761 we'll¦356773 give you the interpretation¦356772.”
\p
\v 5 “\add ≈I've already made my decision\add*,” the¦356776 king¦356776 replied¦356775. “If¦356783 you all can't¦356784 tell¦356777 me¦356785 the¦356786 dream¦356786 and¦356787 \add then give\add* its¦356787 interpretation¦356787, you'll all be¦356789 torn limb from¦356781 limb and¦356790 your¦356790 houses¦356790 made¦356792 into a rubbish heap.
\v 6 But¦356794 if¦356794 you all explain the¦356795 dream¦356795 and¦356796 its¦356796 interpretation¦356796, you'll receive¦356802 gifts¦356798 from¦356803 me, and a reward¦356799 and¦356800 great¦356801 honour¦356800. So¦356794 tell me¦356805 the¦356807 dream¦356807 and¦356808 its¦356808 interpretation¦356808.”
\p
\v 7 “Let¦356814 the king¦356814 tell¦356816 his¦356817 servants¦356817 the dream¦356815,” they¦356813 \add ≈insisted\add*, “and¦356818 we'll¦356819 give the interpretation¦356818.”
\p
\v 8 “I¦356828 know¦356827 for¦356835 certain¦356826 that¦356829 you're all trying to¦356833 gain¦356832 time¦356830,” the¦356841 king¦356822 answered. “\add ≈However, I've made up my mind\add*
\v 9 that¦356843 if¦356844 you all don't¦356847 \add ≈tell me what my dream was\add*, \add ≈then your¦356852 fate is¦356849 already clear\add* because you've \add obviously\add* agreed¦356856 together¦356856 to¦356848 \add ≈keep feeding me with lies¦356854 and¦356855 made-up stories until¦356861 I give in\add*. So then, tell me what \add &my\add* dream was, and¦356869 then I'll¦356869 \add ≈be¦356864 confident\add* that you can give me its¦356871 interpretation¦356871.”
\p
\v 10 “There's¦356883 no one \add ≈in the¦356887 whole world¦356887 who¦356888 could¦356891 tell¦356880\add* the king¦356890 that¦356896,” the \add ≈astrologers¦356909\add* answered¦356880 the king¦356899. “No great¦356900 and¦356901 powerful¦356901 king has¦356905 asked¦356905 any¦356906 magician¦356908 or enchanter or \add ≈astrologer¦356909\add* to¦356906 do that \add before\add*!
\v 11 What¦356924 you're requesting¦356916, \add ≈your majesty\add*, is¦356916 difficult¦356917, and¦356918 \add ≈no human can tell you\add* \add what you dreamt\add*—only¦356925 the¦356924 gods¦356926.”
\v 12 \add ≈That angered the¦356939 king¦356939, and¦356941 he¦356941 got so furious¦356941 that¦356938 he ordered¦356943 for all¦356945 the¦356946 Babylonian wise¦356946 men¦356946 to¦356935 be executed\add*.
