\rem /s1 God Reveals Nebuchadnezzar's Dream
\v 17 Then¦357014 Daniel¦357015 went¦357017 \add back\add* to¦357016 his¦357016 house¦357016 and¦357018 told his companions Hananyah¦357018, Misha'el¦357019, and¦357020 Azaryah¦357020
\v 18 \add ≈so\add* they¦357038 \add ≈might beg\add* for mercy¦357025 from¦357027 the¦357030 god¦357030 of¦357030 the¦357031 heavens¦357031 concerning¦357032 the¦357034 mystery¦357034 \add of the dream\add*, so that¦357035 \add ≈all of them\add* might not¦357037 be executed along with¦357041 the¦357043 rest¦357043 of¦357043 the¦357044 Babylonian wise¦357044 men¦357044.
\v 19 Then¦357047 the mystery¦357053 was¦357054 revealed¦357054 to¦357048 Daniel in¦357049 a vision¦357049 during the¦357052 night¦357052. Aftewrwards, Daniel blessed¦357057 the god¦357058 of¦357050 the heavens¦357059
\v 20 saying¦357063, “Let¦357062 God's¦357068 name¦357065 be¦357064 blessed¦357069 forever and¦357073 ever, because \add all\add* wisdom¦357077 and¦357078 power¦357078 belong to¦357080 him¦357080.
\v 21 He¦357084 moves the¦357086 times¦357086 and¦357087 seasons¦357087 forwards. He¦357088 removes¦357088 \add some\add* kings¦357089 and¦357090 \add ≈gives power to others\add*. It's him who gives wisdom¦357093 to¦357094 the¦357094 wise¦357094 and¦357095 knowledge¦357095 to¦357096 those who have understanding¦357097.
\v 22 He¦357103 reveals¦357100 the¦357101 deep¦357101 and¦357102 hidden¦357102 things¦357101—he¦357103 knows¦357103 what¦357104 is¦357100 in¦357105 the¦357105 darkness¦357105, and¦357106 the¦357106 light¦357106 lives¦357109 with¦357108 him¦357108.
\v 23 Oh¦357132 God¦357113 of¦357113 my¦357114 ancestors¦357114, I¦357117 thank you and praise¦357116 you for¦357111 the¦357119 wisdom¦357119 and¦357120 power¦357120 you¦357121 have¦357121 given¦357121 to¦357122 me¦357122. Now¦357123 you've revealed to¦357124 me¦357124 what we¦357127 asked¦357127 you about¦357113 because you've told us¦357133 what the king¦357132 is¦357115 wanting to¦357133 know.”
\s1 Daniel explains the king's dream
\rem /s1 Daniel Tells the King the Dream and Explains It; Daniel Interprets the Dream
\p
\v 24 So¦357152 Daniel¦357139 went¦357140 to¦357141 Aryok¦357143, the man that¦357138 the king had¦357145 appointed¦357145 to execute Babylon's¦357149 wise men, and¦357152 told¦357153 him¦357155, “Don't¦357158 hurt the wise men in Babylon¦357157. Take¦357161 me¦357161 in to the king and I'll¦357166 tell¦357153 \add *him\add* the \add dream and its\add* interpretation¦357164.”
\p
\v 25 Aryok¦357170 quickly¦357171 took¦357172 Daniel¦357173 in¦357171 to¦357173 the¦357175 king¦357175 and¦357176 told¦357177 him¦357179, “I've¦357182 found¦357182 a man¦357183 among the exiles¦357187 from¦357184 Yehudah¦357189 who¦357190 will¦357193 reveal the interpretation¦357191 \add of your dream\add*.”
\p
\v 26 “Are¦357202 you able¦357204 to¦357205 tell¦357197 me¦357205 the¦357206 dream¦357206 that¦357207 I¦357209 saw¦357209, and¦357210 its¦357210 interpretation¦357210?” the king¦357196 \add ≈asked\add* Daniel¦357198 (also¦357197 called¦357200 Belteshatstsar).
\p
\v 27 “No wise¦357223 men¦357223, enchanters¦357224, magicians¦357225, or astrologers¦357226 are¦357227 able¦357227 to¦357228 do what the¦357229 king¦357229 has demanded,” replied¦357212 Daniel¦357213.
\v 28 “However, there¦357232 is¦357233 a god¦357233 in¦357234 the¦357234 heavens¦357234 who reveals¦357235 mysteries¦357236, and¦357237 he's¦357237 the one who's¦357235 shown you, King¦357238 Nevukadnetstsar¦357239, what¦357240 will¦357242 happen in¦357243 the¦357244 days¦357244 \add ≈to¦357238 come\add*. This¦357251 was your dream¦357245 and¦357246 the visions¦357246 in your mind¦357247 while you were in your bed¦357250:
\v 29 Oh¦357258 king¦357258, \add ≈as you lay there\add* your thoughts¦357259 turned to what¦357264 would¦357266 happen \add ≈in the future\add*, and¦357269 the one who reveals¦357269 mysteries¦357270 showed you what¦357272 is¦357275 going to happen.
\v 30 As for me¦357283, this¦357290 mystery¦357289 was revealed to me, not¦357278 because I'm wiser than¦357284 \add ≈anyone else\add*, but¦357277 so that the¦357298 interpretation¦357298 could be revealed to¦357299 the¦357299 king¦357299, and¦357277 so that you¦357303 can \add ≈understand¦357303 what you saw¦357300 in¦357279\add* your¦357302 mind¦357302.
\p
\v 31 “What you were¦357310 looking at, \add ≈your¦357321 majesty\add*, was an¦357312 incredible¦357314, huge¦357314 statue¦357315. It was¦357317 heavy and¦357318 \add ≈shone brightly\add* as it stood¦357320 \add there\add* \add ≈in front¦357321 of¦357318\add* you¦357321—a terrifying¦357323 sight.
\v 32 The¦357326 statue's¦357326 head¦357327 was¦357328 made of¦357327 pure gold¦357330, its chest¦357332 and¦357333 arms¦357333 were¦357334 silver¦357335, its belly¦357336 and¦357337 thighs¦357337 were¦357338 bronze¦357339,
\v 33 its legs¦357341 were¦357342 made of¦357342 iron¦357343, and its feet¦357344 were¦357347 \add ≈a combination\add* of¦357347 iron¦357348 and¦357349 clay¦357352.
\v 34 You¦357355 continued looking until¦357356 a stone¦357359 was¦357358 \add ≈supernaturally\add* cut¦357358 out¦357358 \add from a mountain\add*, and¦357364 it¦357364 \add tumbled down and\add* struck¦357364 the¦357365 statue¦357365 on¦357363 its¦357368 feet¦357368 of¦357369 iron¦357370 and¦357371 clay¦357371, and¦357372 it¦357372 smashed them¦357373 to pieces.
\v 35 Then the¦357378 iron¦357378, the¦357379 clay¦357379, the¦357380 bronze¦357380, the¦357381 silver¦357381, and¦357382 the¦357382 gold¦357382 were¦357383 crushed¦357376 all¦357393 at the same time and became like¦357384 the \add ≈small pieces of dry straw¦357384 at harvest time in¦357375\add* the summer¦357389. Then the¦357392 wind¦357392 carried¦357390 them away¦357390 so that no trace of them¦357391 could be¦357398 found¦357398, \add ≈but¦357382\add* the¦357400 stone¦357400 that¦357402 struck¦357404 the¦357405 statue¦357405 became¦357406 a¦357407 huge¦357408 mountain¦357407 that then¦357375 filled¦357409 the whole¦357393 world¦357412.
\p
\v 36 “That¦357420 was the dream¦357415, and¦357416 \add now\add* we'll¦357417 tell¦357417 the¦357420 king¦357420 its¦357416 meaning:
\v 37 You¦357425, \add ≈your majesty\add*, are¦357426 the¦357426 king¦357426 of¦357426 kings¦357427 who¦357428 has¦357435 been¦357426 given¦357435 the kingdom¦357431, the¦357432 power¦357432, the¦357433 strength¦357433, and¦357433 the splendour by the god¦357429 of¦357429 the¦357430 heavens¦357430.
\v 38 Wherever people¦357446 live¦357442, he's¦357452 \add ≈placed you in¦357453 charge of¦357453\add* them¦357455, \add and\add* he's \add even\add* put you over¦357455 the animals¦357447 in the¦357448 countryside¦357448 and¦357449 the birds¦357449 in the¦357451 skies¦357451, \add so\add* you're¦357456 the \add statue's\add* gold¦357463 head¦357461.
\v 39 \add ≈But¦357465 another less prominent kingdom will¦357466 follow yours\add*, then¦357465 another¦357468 kingdom¦357474 of¦357478 bronze¦357479 to rule¦357481 over all¦357482 the¦357484 earth¦357484 \add after that\add*.
\v 40 Then¦357486 there'll be¦357489 a fourth¦357487 kingdom¦357486 that's¦357495 strong¦357490 as¦357492 iron—because¦357494 iron¦357496 crushes¦357503 and¦357486 shatters¦357497 all things. Like¦357500 an iron¦357500 \add sledge-hammer\add* that¦357501 smashes things into pieces, it¦357507 will¦357507 smash all those¦357506 others into \add ≈broken\add* pieces.
\v 41 And¦357510 as you¦357512 saw¦357512 the¦357513 feet¦357513 and¦357514 toes¦357514, \add ≈a mixture of iron and potter's¦357521 clay\add*, it will be¦357524 a divided¦357526 kingdom¦357525—some of¦357531 the¦357532 iron's¦357532 strength¦357530 will¦357533 be¦357533 in¦357535 it¦357535, just as¦357536 you¦357540 saw¦357540 iron¦357541 mixed¦357542 with¦357535 the soft clay.
\v 42 As the¦357546 feet¦357547 were¦357550 partly iron¦357550 and¦357551 partly clay¦357553, so that kingdom¦357557 will¦357558 be¦357558 partly strong¦357559 and¦357560 partly \add ≈weak\add*.
\v 43 As¦357583 you¦357566 saw¦357566 the¦357567 iron¦357567 mixed¦357568 with¦357569 soft clay, so \add ≈various peoples will¦357572 make alliances with¦357573 each other\add*, but¦357575 they¦357577 won't¦357575 \add ≈stick¦357578 together\add* just¦357579 as¦357585 iron¦357586 doesn't \add ≈integrate\add* with¦357589 clay¦357591.
\v 44 In¦357593 the days¦357593 of those kings¦357595, the¦357598 god¦357598 of¦357598 the¦357599 heavens¦357599 will¦357604 establish a kingdom¦357605 that¦357601 will¦357609 never be¦357609 destroyed¦357604 or \add ≈taken over by others\add*. It¦357610 will¦357610 crush¦357610 all¦357612 those kingdoms¦357615 and¦357593 put¦357611 an¦357611 end¦357611 to¦357611 them, \add ≈but¦357593\add* it¦357617 will¦357617 stand¦357617 forever¦357618.
\v 45 Just as¦357620 you¦357625 saw¦357625 that¦357626 a stone¦357629 was¦357628 \add ≈supernaturally\add* cut¦357628 out¦357628 of¦357652 the mountain¦357627, and¦357634 that it¦357634 crushed¦357634 the¦357635 iron¦357635, the¦357636 bronze¦357636, the¦357637 clay¦357637, the¦357638 silver¦357638, and¦357639 the¦357639 gold¦357639, the \add ≈powerful¦357641\add* God¦357640 has¦357642 revealed to¦357643 the¦357643 king¦357643 what¦357644 will¦357646 happen \add ≈in¦357633 the future\add*. The¦357627 dream¦357650 is true, and its¦357652 interpretation¦357652 is trustworthy¦357651.”
\s1 Daniel is rewarded by the king
\rem /s1 Daniel and His Friends Promoted; The King Rewards Daniel; Nebuchadnezzar Rewards Daniel
\p
\v 46 Then¦357655 King¦357656 Nevukadnetstsar¦357657 fell¦357658 \add ≈to his knees with his face¦357661 to the¦357656 ground to¦357662 worship¦357663\add* Daniel¦357662, and¦357664 he¦357666 commanded that a \add burnt\add* offering¦357664 and¦357665 incense¦357665 be offered¦357667 up to¦357668 him¦357668.
\v 47 The¦357671 king¦357671 said to¦357672 Daniel¦357672, “It's true that¦357690 your god¦357678 is¦357680 the¦357680 god¦357680 of¦357680 gods¦357681 and¦357682 the¦357682 master¦357682 of masters¦357682, and¦357684 the one¦357684 who¦357684 reveals¦357684 mysteries¦357689, because you've¦357687 been¦357687 able¦357687 to¦357688 reveal¦357688 this¦357690 mystery¦357689.”
\v 48 Then¦357692 the¦357693 king¦357693 gave Daniel¦357694 high honours and¦357696 gave¦357699 him¦357701 many¦357704 great¦357695 gifts¦357696, and¦357702 he¦357702 made¦357702 him¦357702 ruler¦357702 over¦357703 the whole¦357704 province¦357706 of Babylon¦357715 and chief¦357708 supervisor over¦357711 all¦357712 of Babylon's¦357707 wise¦357714 men¦357714.
\v 49 Daniel \add ≈asked¦357718 the¦357721 king¦357721 to appoint¦357722\add* Shadrak¦357728 \add !(Hananyah¦357728)\add*, Meyshak¦357728 \add !(Misha'el¦357728)\add*, and¦357717 Avednego \add !(Azaryah)\add* over¦357723 the¦357724 administration¦357724 of¦357725 the¦357726 Babylonian province¦357726, \add ≈but¦357717\add* Daniel remained at¦357733 the king's¦357734 court¦357733.
\c 3
