\p
\v 8 \add ≈But¦357901\add* some Chaldean¦357900 \add astrologers\add* \add ≈went to the king to make trouble for\add* the Jews¦357904.
\v 9 They¦357907 reported to¦357908 King¦357909 Nevukadnetstsar¦357908, “Long¦357910 live¦357912 the¦357909 king¦357909.
\v 10 Oh¦357916 king¦357916, you've made¦357917 a decree¦357918 that¦357919 every¦357920 man¦357922 who¦357923 hears¦357925 the¦357926 sound¦357926 of¦357920 the \add ≈musical¦357937 instruments\add*, must¦357925 bow down¦357938 and¦357939 worship¦357939 the gold¦357941 statue¦357940,
\v 11 \add ≈but¦357943\add* anyone¦357943 who¦357945 doesn't bow and¦357949 worship¦357949 will¦357948 be¦357950 thrown¦357950 into¦357951 a fiery¦357955 furnace¦357953.
\v 12 \add Now,\add* there¦357957 are¦357958 certain Jews who¦357960 you've appointed¦357962 over¦357964 the¦357966 administration¦357966 of¦357966 the¦357967 province¦357967 of¦357967 Babylon¦357968: Shadrak¦357969, Meyshak, and¦357986 Avednego. These¦357974 men¦357958, \add ≈your¦357982 majesty\add*, \add ≈don't take any notice of¦357986\add* you¦357989: they¦357991 don't serve your gods¦357982, or worship¦357991 the gold¦357987 statue¦357986 that¦357988 you've set¦357989 up¦357989.”
\p
\v 13 Then¦357994 Nevukadnetstsar¦357995 in¦357996 a furious¦357997 rage¦357996 commanded that Shadrak¦358000 \add !(Hananyah¦358000)\add*, Meyshak¦358000 \add !(Misha'el¦358000)\add*, and¦357997 Avednego \add !(Azaryah)\add* be¦358007 \add ≈arrested\add*. Then \add *they¦358007\add* were¦358007 brought¦358007 to¦357999 the¦358009 king¦358009
\v 14 \add ≈who questioned\add* them¦358014, “Shadrak¦358016, Meyshak, and¦358024 Avednego, is it true that you won't¦358021 serve my¦358020 gods¦358020 or worship¦358029 the¦358024 gold¦358025 statue¦358024 that¦358026 I¦358027 set¦358027 up¦358027?
\v 15 Now¦358031 if¦358032 you're ready¦358034, as soon as you hear¦358039 the \add ≈musical¦358052 instruments\add* of¦358040 music¦358052, if you all¦358049 bow down¦358053 and¦358054 worship¦358054 the¦358055 statue¦358055 I¦358058 \add ≈erected\add*, \add ≈everything¦358049 will¦358034 be okay\add*. But¦358048 if¦358059 you don't¦358060, you'll immediately be¦358065 thrown¦358065 into¦358066 a blazing¦358070 hot furnace¦358068. \add ≈Then what god¦358074 could rescue¦358077 you from¦358078 my¦358080 punishment\add*?”
\p
\v 16 “Oh¦358089 Nevukadnetstsar¦358089,” \add *the¦358090 three¦358090 of¦358090 them¦358090\add* answered¦358087 the¦358088 king¦358088, “we¦358093 don't¦358090 need¦358092 to¦358088 answer \add that last question\add*.
\v 17 \add ≈If¦358117 that's what happens to us\add*, the god¦358102 that we¦358105 serve is¦358102 able¦358107 to¦358108 rescue¦358108 us¦358108 from¦358109 the blazing¦358113 fire¦358112 \add ≈and¦358114 from¦358114 your¦358116 punishment. He¦358118 will¦358118 save us,\add* \add ≈your majesty\add*.
\v 18 \add ≈But¦358126 just so it's¦358123 clear\add* \add ≈your majesty\add*, even if¦358120 he doesn't, we¦358133 won't¦358121 serve your gods¦358128 or worship¦358141 that¦358138 gold¦358137 statue¦358136.”
\s1 Shadrak, Meyshak, and Avednego thrown into the fire
\rem /s1 The Fiery Furnace; Daniel's Three Friends Are Sentenced to Death; The Blazing Furnace
\p
\v 19 \add ≈That¦358166 made Nevukadnetstsar¦358145 absolutely furious¦358147 and¦358148 it¦358150 could be seen in¦358144 his¦358149 face¦358149\add*. He¦358158 ordered¦358159 that¦358166 the¦358148 furnace¦358161 be made seven¦358164 times hotter \add ≈than usual\add*
\v 20 and¦358170 commanded \add ≈some of¦358171 his¦358175 top warriors¦358170\add* to tie¦358177 up Shadrak¦358178, Meyshak, and Avednego and then¦358170 to throw¦358182 them into¦358183 the¦358183 blazing¦358185 furnace¦358183.
\v 21 \add ≈So while still fully dressed even with¦358191 their¦358192 turbans¦358195 still on¦358191,\add* they¦358190 were¦358190 tied¦358190 up and¦358195 thrown¦358197 into¦358198 the¦358198 middle¦358198 of¦358198 the¦358200 furnace¦358200 where the¦358201 fire¦358201 was blazing¦358202.
\v 22 \add Unfortunately\add*, because¦358206 of¦358211 the¦358212 king's¦358212 insistent command, the¦358214 furnace¦358214 was¦358215 extremely hot and¦358214 the flames¦358227 killed¦358225 the men¦358217 who¦358219 \add ≈threw them¦358226 in\add*,
\v 23 \add ≈but¦358231\add* Shadrak¦358234, Meyshak, and¦358236 Avednego fell¦358238 into the¦358231 \add ≈roaring flames¦358243 while still securely tied¦358245 up\add*.
\p
\v 24 Then¦358248 King¦358250 Nevukadnetstsar¦358249 was¦358251 startled and jumped up and asked¦358266 his¦358256 counsellors¦358256, “Wasn't¦358269 it three¦358259 \add ≈who were tied¦358264 up\add* that were thrown¦358260 \add there\add* into¦358261 the¦358261 middle¦358261 of¦358261 the¦358263 fire¦358263?
\p “Certainly¦358268, \add ≈your majesty\add*,” they¦358265 replied¦358266.
\p
\v 25 “Look¦358273 \add there, then\add*!” he¦358271 responded. “I¦358275 can see¦358276 \em four¦358278\em* men¦358277 walking¦358280 in¦358281 the¦358281 middle¦358281 of¦358281 the¦358283 fire¦358283, and they're not tied up and they're not hurt¦358284, and¦358289 the¦358291 fourth¦358291 one looks¦358273 like¦358293 a¦358294 \add ≈young¦358294 god¦358296\add*.”
\rem /s1 The Three Men Are Released and Promoted
\p
\v 26 Then¦358299 Nevukadnetstsar¦358301 went¦358319 over by the furnace¦358303 door¦358302 and¦358307 \add ≈called¦358319 out\add*, “Shadrak, Meyshak, and Avednego, servants¦358313 of the Most¦358317 High¦358317 God¦358316, come¦358319 out¦358319, and¦358320 come¦358320 over here!” Then¦358321 \add *the¦358323 three¦358323 men¦358323\add* walked out¦358322 from¦358327 the¦358329 middle¦358329 of¦358329 the¦358330 fire¦358330.
\v 27 \add ≈All the king's¦358337 officials\add* gathered¦358332 \add ≈around¦358332 them\add* and saw¦358338 that¦358341 the¦358345 fire¦358345 \add ≈hadn't¦358358 harmed¦358358 them\add*—their hair¦358347 wasn't even singed¦358350, their cloaks weren't damaged, and \add ≈they¦358353 didn't¦358342 even smell¦358354 like smoke\add*.
\p
\v 28 Nevukadnetstsar¦358361 \add ≈spoke\add*, “\add ≈Praise\add* the god¦358364 of¦358365 Shadrak¦358367, Meyshak, and Avednego. He¦358375 sent¦358373 his angel¦358374 and rescued¦358375 his¦358376 servants¦358376 who¦358377 \add ≈had put their trust¦358378\add* in him¦358379. They¦358378 \add ≈took objection to¦358376\add* the king's¦358381 command¦358380 \add ≈at¦358364 the cost¦358364 of¦358364 their own¦358364 lives¦358364\add*, rather than serve or worship¦358393 any¦358394 god¦358396 except¦358397 their¦358398 god¦358398.
\v 29 I \add ≈hereby\add* make a decree¦358402 that¦358403 any¦358404 people¦358406, nation¦358407, or language¦358408 that¦358409 speaks anything offensive against¦358414 the god¦358415 of¦358416 Shadrak¦358418, Meyshak, and Avednego will¦358411 have their¦358415 houses¦358424 made¦358423 into \add #rubbish heaps\add* and¦358424 they'll \add ≈be¦358426 torn limb from¦358400 limb\add*, because¦358429 there's¦358432 no other god¦358433 who's¦358435 able¦358437 to¦358438 save \add people\add* like¦358439 that.”
\p
\v 30 Then¦358441 the¦358442 king¦358442 \add ≈gave\add* Shadrak¦358444 \add !(Hananyah¦358444)\add*, Meyshak¦358444 \add !(Misha'el¦358444)\add*, and¦358446 Avednego \add !(Azaryah)\add* \add ≈more important positions in¦358448 Babylon¦358449 province¦358448 than they had before\add*.
\c 4
\s1 Nevukadnetstsar's second dream
\rem /s1 Nebuchadnezzar's Dream of a Tree; Nebuchadnezzar's Second Dream; Nebuchadnezzar's madness; Nebuchadnezzar's Dream about a Tree
\p
\v 1 King¦358501 Nevukadnetstsar¦358501 \add !(Nebuchadnezzar¦358501)\add* \add sent this message\add* to all people groups, nations, and¦358505 languages that live all over the world:
\p May you \add ≈have peace and prosperity¦358505\add*.
\v 2 It¦358511 seemed good to me¦358511 to \add ≈let you all know about¦358515\add* the \add ≈amazing miracles\add* that the \add ≈supreme\add* God¦358510 has done for me¦358518.
\q1
\v 3 The¦358527 miracles he does are incredible,
\q1 ≈and¦358521 his acts of¦358527 creation are powerful.
\q1 His¦358528 kingdom will¦358533 last forever,
\q1 ≈and his authority goes from¦358521 one generation to¦358533 another.
\p
\v 4 I¦358546, Nevukadnetstsar¦358541, was¦358545 \add ≈doing well at home\add*, and¦358548 prospering \add at my work\add* in¦358536 my palace,
\v 5 \add ≈but¦358555\add* \add one night\add* as I lay in¦358557 bed, I had a¦358567 dream¦358572 that scared me—the images and¦358566 visions in my¦358565 head terrified me.
\v 6 \add ≈So I¦358582 summoned\add* all¦358589 of Babylon's wise men to¦358595 come and¦358589 tell¦358602 me the interpretation¦358601 of the dream¦358597.
\v 7 \add ≈When\add* those magicians, fortune-tellers, sorcerers and¦358605 astrologers arrived, I¦358611 told them the dream, but¦358605 they didn't know the interpretation.
\p
\v 8 Finally, Daniel came in—he'd been named Belteshatstsar \add !(Belteshazzar)\add* after my god's name. The¦358621 spirit of¦358623 the¦358625 holy gods is in him, and¦358626 I told him the dream:
\v 9 “Oh Belteshatstsar, chief of¦358633 the magicians, since I know that the spirit of¦358635 the holy gods is in¦358640 you and that no mystery is too difficult for¦358638 you, \add ≈go through\add* the visions of my dream and tell me their interpretation.
\p
\v 10 This is the¦358660 vision¦358660 that was in my¦358661 mind¦358661 as a lay in bed¦358664: I¦358659 was looking and¦358665 was surprised to see¦358665 \add ≈a very tall\add* tree growing in the middle of¦358660 \add ≈a field\add*.
\v 11 The¦358679 tree¦358679 kept growing taller and¦358680 stronger \add ≈until\add* its¦358681 top reached the sky and¦358684 it¦358686 \add ≈could be seen from¦358688 all over the¦358691 world\add*.
\v 12 It had beautiful leaves¦358701 were¦358713 beautiful and lots of fruit on¦358700 it. It \add provided\add* food for everything, the¦358715 animals¦358715 in the countryside¦358709 found shade under it, and¦358710 the¦358710 birds of¦358710 the¦358711 sky¦358711 lived in its¦358699 branches. \add ≈Every living thing benefitted\add* from it¦358712.
\p
\v 13 I was looking at the visions in my mind¦358721 as I lay in bed, and¦358727 then, wow, a holy sentinel came down from¦358722 the heavens.
\v 14 He¦358761 shouted loudly, ‘Chop that¦358746 tree down and¦358758 cut its branches off, strip off its leaves, and¦358763 scatter its fruit. Let the animals \add ≈run away\add* from under it¦358766 and the birds from its branches.
\v 15 But¦358776 leave the stump with its¦358779 roots in the ground, and bind it with a band of iron and bronze. Let it become wet with the dew in the tender grass of the field, \add ≈accompanied only by those animals that¦358770 live\add* in the grass.
\v 16 Let¦358826 his¦358811 mind\f + \fr 4:16 \ft It seems now that the tree represented a man.\f* be¦358833 changed from a man¦358815 to an animal as seven periods pass over him¦358817.
\v 17 \add &That\add* sentence is by the decree of¦358846 the¦358848 sentinels, and¦358849 the¦358849 decision is a command of¦358849 the holy ones, so that the living may know that the highest one is ruler over human \add #kingdoms\add* and gives \add #them\add* to whoever he wants—even to those \add ≈who seem unimportant\add*.’
\p
\v 18 I, King Nevukadnetstsar, had this dream. Now you, Belteshatstsar, tell me the interpretation, because none of¦358855 the wise men in my kingdom can tell me the interpretation. But¦358855 you can, because¦358860 the¦358865 spirit of the¦358866 holy gods is in¦358867 you.”
\s1 The saving/explaining of Daniel of dream of King
\rem /s1 Daniel Explains the Dream; Daniel Interprets the Second Dream; Daniel Interprets the Dream
\p
\v 19 Daniel (also¦358880 known as Belteshatstsar) was appalled for a while \add at the meaning of the dream\add*, and \add ≈worried how to¦358886 present it¦358876\add*. The¦358884 king¦358877 \add saw this\add* and said, Belteshatstsar, don't \add ≈hold back on being honest about¦358881\add* the dream its interpretation.”
\p “My master,” \add *he\add* answered, “\add ≈If only\add* the¦358877 dream was for those who¦358878 hate you¦358873, and¦358883 its interpretation was for your¦358881 enemies.
\v 20 You¦358892 saw¦358891 that tree¦358902 that grew and became¦358919 strong, and whose top reached the sky and was¦358920 visible all \add ≈over¦358931 the world\add*,
\v 21 with its beautiful leaves and¦358937 plentiful fruit. \add ≈It¦358942 provided\add* food for everyone, and the animals from the countryside lived under it¦358940, and the¦358947 birds lived in its branches.
\v 22 That¦358977 was you¦358950, \add ≈your majesty\add*. You've grown and become strong—your greatness has¦358982 grown and reaches to heaven¦358967, and your dominion to the ends of¦358986 the¦358987 earth.
\v 23 \add ≈Then\add* the king¦359002 saw a holy sentinel coming down from¦359005 heaven¦359011 and saying¦358996, ‘Chop down the tree¦359001 and destroy it, but¦358995 leave¦358997 the¦358998 stump¦358998 and the roots¦358999 in the ground, bound with a band of¦359000 iron and bronze, in the¦359001 tender grass of¦359002 the countryside, and let him be¦359004 wet with the dew, and let \add ≈him live\add* with the animals of the fields until seven periods of time pass over him.’
\p
\v 24 “\add ≈Your¦359020 majesty\add*, this is the interpretation: It's¦359017 \add actually\add* the decree of¦359020 what the supreme God¦359015 has declared will¦359028 happen to¦359025 \add you\add*, my¦359016 master the king¦359015.
\v 25 You will be driven \add ≈away from society\add* and you'll live with the animals in the countryside. You'll be forced to eat grass like a bull, and you'll \add sleep on the ground and\add* be wet with dew \add in the morning\add* for seven \add ≈years\add* until you acknowledge that the highest one is ruler over \add ≈humankind\add* and \add ≡learn that\add* he gives kingdoms to whoever he wants.
\v 26 \add ≈But¦359052\add* \add just\add* as it was¦359054 commanded to leave the stump of¦359042 the tree with its roots, \add so too\add* your kingdom¦359050 will be¦359054 restored to you from the time you recognise that heaven rules \add over earth\add*.
\v 27 Therefore, \add ≈your majesty¦359075\add*, \add ≈please consider\add* my¦359073 advice: \add ≈stop sinning and do what is¦359061 right\add*, and \add ≡stop\add* \add ≈disobeying God by¦359072 starting to\add* show mercy to the¦359072 oppressed, so that¦359066 perhaps your prosperity \add ≈might¦359072 be prolonged\add*.”
\rem /s1 The Dream's Fulfillment; Nebuchadnezzar's Humiliation; The Dream Is Fulfilled
\p
\v 28 \add But\add* all of¦359080 that did happen to¦359087 King¦359081 Nevukadnetstsar¦359089.
\v 29 Twelve months \add ≈later,\add* he¦359130 was walking on¦359125 \add the upper terrace of\add* the¦359125 royal palace in¦359125 Babylon
\v 30 \add ≈when\add* \add *he¦359145\add* \add looked across\add* and said, “Isn't that¦359154 the great Babylon, which I've built as¦359147 a royal residence by the strength of my power and for the glory of my majesty?”
\p
\v 31 \add ≈He'd only just finished¦359162 saying that¦359180 when he heard a voice in\add* the¦359162 sky¦359168, “It's¦359172 \add now\add* decreed to¦359162 you, King¦359184 Nebuchadnezzar¦359165: you've just lost your kingdom¦359184.
\v 32 You'll be¦359196 driven away from \add other\add* people, and¦359198 \add ≈you'll live\add* will be¦359199 with¦359200 the¦359200 animals \add out\add* in¦359200 the countryside \add where\add* you'll \add ≈have¦359214 to\add* eat grass like a cow. Seven \add ≈years will pass before\add* you¦359214 acknowledge that the highest one is¦359207 ruler over mankind and he gives \add @kingdoms¦359198\add* to whoever he wants.”
\p
\v 33 Immediately \add ≈what had been said in¦359217 advance happened to¦359223\add* Nebuchadnezzar. He was driven away from \add ≈society\add*. He ate grass like a cow, and his body was wet with¦359217 dew \add each morning after sleeping on the ground\add*. \add ≈Eventually\add* his hair had grown long like eagles' feathers, and his nails \add ≡were long\add* like birds' claws.
\s1 Nevukadnetstsar praises God
\rem /s1 Nebuchadnezzar Praises God
\p
\v 34 “After \add the seven years\add*, I, Nevukadnetstsar¦359246 \add ≈looked up¦359248 toward\add* heaven¦359251 and my reason returned to¦359263 me. Then I praised¦359247 and honoured¦359249 the highest one who¦359259 lives forever, because his authority is¦359262 everlasting and his kingdom endures \add ≈through the generations\add*.
\v 35 \add Compared to him,\add* all the inhabitants of the earth are \add ≈insignificant like ants\add*—he \add ≈issues his commands\add* to the heavenly army and to earth's inhabitants. No one can hold back his hand or \add ≈has any right to ask\add* him, ‘What have you done?’ ”
\p
\v 36 “At that time my sanity returned to me, and I was reestablished in my kingdom. My majesty and my splendour was restored to me \add ≈as I became again the respected leader\add* of my kingdom. My counsellors and my nobles \add ≈wanted to be in my company\add*, and \add ≈I became even more powerful than I was before\add*.
\v 37 Now I, Nevukadnetstsar, praise, extol, and honour the king of the heavens, because \add ≈everything he does is\add*, and his \add ≈methods\add* are just, and he's able to humble those who \add ≈become proud\add*.
\c 5
\s1 The writing on the wall
\rem /s1 Belshazzar's Banquet; The writing on the wall; Belshazzar's Feast; The Writing on the Wall
\p
\v 1 \add Several years later when\add* Belshatstsar \add was\add* king¦359267, he¦359268 put on a feast¦359269 for¦359271 a thousand¦359272 of¦359271 his¦359271 nobles¦359271, and¦359273 he¦359276 was¦359276 drinking¦359276 wine¦359275 with them \add *all\add*.
\v 2 \add ≈As Belshatstsar was¦359295 drinking¦359296\add* the wine¦359282, he¦359279 \add remembered\add* the gold¦359285 and¦359286 silver¦359286 cups that¦359287 his father¦359290 Nevukadnetstsar¦359289 had¦359288 taken¦359288 from¦359291 the¦359293 temple¦359293 in Yerushalem¦359295, \add and\add* ordered¦359279 that they¦359296 be¦359295 brought¦359288 in so¦359296 that the¦359298 king¦359298 and¦359299 his nobles¦359299, and his¦359299 wives¦359300 and his concubines¦359301, could¦359296 drink¦359296 from them¦359297.
\v 3 \add ≈So¦359320\add* they¦359304 brought¦359304 in the gold¦359306 cups that¦359307 had¦359308 been taken out¦359308 of¦359314 God's¦359315 residence in¦359317 Yerushalem¦359317, then¦359303 the king¦359320 and¦359321 his nobles¦359321, his¦359321 wives¦359322 and his concubines¦359323, drank¦359318 from¦359309 them¦359319.
\v 4 They¦359325 drank¦359325 the¦359326 wine¦359326 and¦359327 praised¦359327 the¦359328 gods¦359328 of¦359328 gold¦359329 and¦359330 silver¦359330, bronze¦359331, iron¦359332, wood¦359333, and¦359334 stone¦359334.
\rem /s1 The Writing on the Wall
\p
\v 5 Suddenly, the¦359338 fingers¦359341 of¦359342 a¦359343 human¦359345 hand appeared and¦359346 started writing¦359346 on¦359349 the¦359351 plaster¦359351 on the king's¦359357 palace¦359355 wall¦359354, opposite the lampstand¦359348. \add When\add* the king¦359358 saw¦359359 the¦359360 back of¦359360 the¦359361 hand¦359361 that¦359362 was¦359363 writing¦359363,
\v 6 he \add ≈was very frightened and the¦359372 colour drained from his face¦359367\add*. His¦359366 \add ≈legs felt weak\add* and his knees¦359375 started knocking¦359378 \add together\add*,
\v 7 \add and\add* \add *he¦359381\add* called¦359380 out for the¦359384 enchanters¦359384, the¦359385 Chaldeans, and¦359387 the¦359387 astrologers¦359387 to¦359404 be brought¦359383 in¦359383. The¦359389 king¦359389 told those Babylonian wise men¦359397, “Anyone who¦359398 can read¦359400 this¦359402 writing¦359401 and tell¦359390 me¦359404 its¦359403 interpretation¦359403 will be¦359406 \add ≈dressed¦359406 in royal robes\add* and will have a chain¦359407 of¦359415 gold¦359412 \add placed\add* around his¦359415 neck¦359415, and he'll¦359400 become the third¦359416 ruler¦359418 in the kingdom¦359417.”
\v 8 \add ≈When¦359426\add* all¦359424 the¦359425 king's¦359426 wise¦359425 men¦359425 of¦359425 the¦359426 king \add ≈got there\add*, \add ≈none of them\add* could¦359429 read¦359431 the writing¦359430 or \add ≈tell the¦359435 king¦359435 what it meant\add*.
\v 9 Then¦359437 King¦359438 Belshatstsar¦359439 became more afraid, his face¦359442 \add ≈went whiter still\add*, and¦359445 his nobles¦359445 \add also\add* \add ≈had no idea what to do\add*.
\p
\v 10 \add My now\add* the¦359448 queen¦359448\f + \fr 5:10 \ft Possibly ‘the previous queen’, i.e., the queen mother (as the next verse displays her knowledge of past events).\f* had heard about¦359450 what the¦359451 king¦359451 and¦359452 his¦359452 noblemen¦359452 had been¦359470 saying¦359459, so she¦359455 came¦359455 into the banquet¦359454 house¦359453 and¦359459 said¦359459, “Long live¦359462 the king. Don't let¦359465 \add ≈this get under your¦359467 skin\add*. Don't let \add ≈it drain you¦359465\add*.
\v 11 There's¦359472 a¦359473 man¦359473 in¦359474 your kingdom¦359474 who¦359475 has the spirit¦359476 of¦359476 the holy¦359478 gods in¦359479 him¦359479. In¦359480 the¦359480 days¦359480 of¦359480 your¦359481 father¦359481, this man has illumination¦359482 and¦359483 insight¦359483 and¦359484 wisdom¦359484 like¦359485 the¦359485 wisdom¦359485 of the gods. Your¦359500 father King¦359490 Nevukadnetstsar¦359491 appointed¦359498 him chief¦359493 of¦359493 the¦359494 magicians¦359494, sorcerers, Chaldeans¦359496, and astrologers¦359497,
\v 12 because¦359504 this Daniel had an¦359511 excellent spirit¦359506, knowledge¦359509, and¦359510 insight¦359510 for interpreting¦359511 dreams¦359512, explaining¦359513 riddles¦359514, and¦359515 solving problems. \add ≈So summon¦359529\add* Daniel who the king¦359522 named¦359525 Belteshatstsar and¦359530 he'll¦359531 tell \add >you\add* the interpretation¦359530.”
\rem /s1 Daniel Explains the Writing; The Writing on the Wall Interpreted
\p
\v 13 \add ≈So\add* Daniel was¦359536 brought in¦359534 to the king \add ≈who¦359548\add* said to \add *him\add*, “\add So,\add* you're that Daniel—one¦359550 of¦359550 the exiles¦359553 that my¦359559 father¦359559 the king brought from¦359560 Yehudah¦359555.
\v 14 I've¦359564 heard¦359564 about¦359565 you¦359565—\add ≡heard\add* that¦359567 the spirit¦359568 of¦359568 the gods¦359569 is¦359575 in you, and that \add ≈you have¦359564\add* illumination¦359571 and¦359572 insight¦359572 and¦359573 excellent wisdom¦359573.
\v 15 Now¦359578 the¦359581 wise¦359581 men¦359581 and enchanters¦359582 were¦359592 brought¦359579 in \add ≈here\add* to¦359596 read¦359587 this¦359586 writing¦359585 \add on the wall\add* and \add ≈tell\add* me¦359580 its¦359588 interpretation¦359588, but¦359578 they¦359592 couldn't \add ≈interpret¦359593 it\add*.
\v 16 But¦359598 I've¦359599 heard¦359599 about¦359600 you¦359600—that¦359602 you're able¦359604 to¦359608 give¦359608 interpretations¦359607 and¦359609 solve¦359610 problems¦359609. Now¦359611, if¦359612 you're able¦359613 to¦359616 read¦359616 \add &that\add* writing¦359615 and¦359617 \add ≈tell me¦359618 what it means\add*, you'll be¦359620 clothed¦359620 with¦359631 \add ≈kingly¦359619 robes\add* and have¦359599 a chain¦359621 of¦359624 gold¦359626 \add placed\add* around your¦359629 neck¦359629, and¦359630 you'll be \add made\add* the third¦359630\add -highest\add* ruler¦359632 in¦359631 the¦359631 kingdom¦359631.”
\p
\v 17 “\add ≈I'm not after\add* your gifts¦359641, and your rewards¦359644 \add ≈can be¦359643 given¦359646 to someone¦359645 else,\add*” Daniel¦359637 \add ≈responded\add*. “But¦359638, I'll read¦359649 the writing¦359648 to¦359650 the¦359650 king¦359650 and \add ≡I'll\add* \add ≈tell¦359638 you\add* the interpretation¦359651.
\p
\v 18 “As for you¦359654, \add ≈your majesty\add*, the highest god¦359657 gave¦359664 the kingship, power, honour¦359662, and¦359661 majesty¦359663, to¦359665 your father¦359666 Nevukadnetstsar¦359665.
\v 19 Because of¦359675 \add >that¦359671\add* power, all¦359675 peoples¦359676, nations¦359677, and¦359678 ethnic \add groups\add* trembled¦359680 and \add ≈were¦359679 afraid¦359682 when his¦359685 army approached\add*. He killed¦359691 whoever he wanted, and he kept alive¦359697 those he wanted. He \add ≈promoted\add* whoever he wanted, and he humiliated \add ≈others when he wanted to¦359674\add*.
\v 20 But¦359711 when \add ≈he became proud¦359716 and stubborn\add* and started acting¦359716 presumptuously¦359716, he¦359717 was \add ≈disqualified\add* from¦359718 the¦359720 throne¦359720, and his¦359713 \add ≈splendid reputation was deflated\add*.
\v 21 He¦359730 was¦359730 driven \add ≈right out of¦359731 the¦359735 community\add*, and \add ≈he started behaving like\add* an animal¦359735—he \add ended up\add* living¦359742 with wild¦359741 donkeys¦359741 and eating grass¦359743 like¦359744 a cow. \add He slept on the ground, and in the morning\add* his¦359748 body¦359748 was wet¦359749 with dew¦359746, until¦359750 he¦359753 \add finally\add* recognized that¦359754 the¦359757 highest god¦359757 is ruler over¦359760 \add ≈all\add* mankind¦359761 and¦359762 that he \add ≈appoints¦359766 whoever he wants as leaders over the kingdoms¦359760\add*.
\p
\v 22 “Yet you¦359770, Belshatstsar, his¦359772 son¦359772, haven't humbled¦359775 \add ≈yourself despite already knowing¦359784 all¦359781 that¦359783\add*.
\v 23 \add On the contrary\add*, you've¦359791 \add ≈proudly promoted\add* yourself¦359791 \add ≈as being greater than\add* the master¦359787 of¦359787 the¦359789 heavens¦359789. \add ≈You¦359791 brought¦359796 the temple¦359795 cups in¦359808 here\add* and you and your nobles¦359801, your¦359801 wives¦359804 and your concubines¦359805, have¦359791 drunk¦359807 wine¦359806 from them¦359808. You praised¦359826 the gods¦359809 \add ≈made from\add* silver¦359810 and¦359812 gold¦359812, bronze¦359813, iron¦359814, wood¦359815, and¦359816 stone¦359816, which can't¦359818 see¦359820, or hear¦359823, or know¦359825 \add >anything¦359825\add*, yet you haven't honoured the¦359827 god¦359827 \add ≈who¦359828 decides your future and every breath¦359830 that you¦359837 take\add*.
\p
\v 24 “\add So\add* then¦359839 \add its\add* from¦359840 his presence¦359842 \add that\add* the¦359845 palm¦359845 of¦359846 a hand¦359848 was sent¦359843, and¦359849 this¦359850 \add ≈message was written¦359849\add*.
\v 25 This¦359853 \add ≈is¦359854 how it¦359856 reads\add*, ‘Numbered, numbered, weighed, and¦359860 divided,’
\v 26 \add and\add* this¦359862 is¦359863 what it¦359871 means:
\li1 \bd Numbered¦359867\bd*: God¦359869 has¦359867 numbered¦359867 \add ≈your¦359870 days of¦359870 leadership\add* and¦359871 brought¦359871 \add #them\add* to an end¦359871,
\li1
\v 27 \bd Weighed¦359874\bd*: You've been¦359874 weighed¦359874 on¦359875 the¦359875 scales¦359875 and¦359876 found¦359876 deficient, \add and\add*
\li1
\v 28 \bd Divided\bd*: Your¦359881 kingdom¦359881 has¦359882 been¦359882 divided and given¦359882 to¦359883 \add the\add* Medes¦359883 and¦359884 \add the\add* Persians.”
\p
\v 29 Then¦359886 Belshatstsar¦359889 \add ≈gave an order¦359888\add*, and¦359890 they¦359890 \add ≈placed a purple¦359892 robe over Daniel's¦359891 shoulders\add* and¦359893 a gold¦359897 chain¦359893 was¦359898 \add ≈placed\add* around his¦359900 neck¦359900, and¦359901 an \add official\add* announcement¦359901 was made¦359901 that¦359903 he¦359905 would¦359905 \add now\add* be¦359905 the third¦359907\add -highest\add* ruler¦359906 in¦359908 the¦359908 kingdom¦359908.
\p
\v 30 \add However,\add* the¦359911 Chaldean King¦359914 Belshatstsar was¦359912 killed¦359912 that \add ≈very\add* night¦359911,
\v 31 and Dareyavesh \add !(Darius)\add* the Mede \add ≈became the new ruler at an age of\add* around sixty-two.
\c 6
\s1 Daniel gets fed to the lions
\rem /s1 Daniel in the Den of Lions; The Plot against Daniel; Daniel in the lion-pit; Daniel in the Lions' Den; Daniel in the Pit of Lions
\p
\v 1 \add The new King\add* Dareyavesh¦359920 \add !(Darius¦359920)\add* \add ≈decided to divide the¦359925 kingdom¦359925 into 120 provinces with a governor over each one\add*.
\v 2 Over¦359936 them were three higher officials (Daniel¦359934 was one¦359940 of them) that those governors were accountable to so that the¦359946 king \add ≈wouldn't be¦359943 ripped off\add*,
\v 3 \add ≈but¦359949\add* because Daniel¦359954 was¦359955 exceptionally talented, he¦359969 became noticed above¦359949 the \add other\add* high officials¦359951 and governors¦359951, and¦359966 the¦359966 king¦359966 \add ≈decided\add* to¦359964 appoint him over the¦359965 whole kingdom.
\v 4 \add ≈That¦359985 led\add* the high officials¦359980 and the governors¦359980 to try to find \add ≈some fault with\add* Daniel¦359974 with regard to \add ≈his administrative¦359980 duties\add*. However, they weren't able to find any \add ≈misdemeanours or corruption that¦359975 he¦359976 could be accused of¦359994\add* because¦359984 he was¦359976 \add ^honest, reliable, and diligent\add*.
\v 5 Then¦359999 these men \add ≈concluded\add*, “We won't¦360013 find any grounds for¦360006 \add making an\add* accusation against this Daniel¦360006 unless we find¦360016 something against him¦360030 to¦360006 do with the¦360007 \add #laws\add* of¦360007 his god.”
\p
\v 6 \add ≈So\add* those¦360035 high officials and governors \add made a plan and\add* went as a group to¦360040 the¦360048 king and \add ≈made this¦360041 suggestion\add*, “\add ≈Long live your majesty\add*, King Dareyavesh.
\v 7 All the¦360054 high officials¦360054 of the kingdom, the \add ≈various leaders\add*, the counsellors, and the governors¦360054 have consulted together that the king¦360065 should establish and enforce a law that for¦360066 thirty days, anyone who makes a request of any god or \add ≡of any\add* man other than you, \add ≈your majesty\add*, shall be¦360062 thrown into¦360063 the¦360065 lions' den.
\v 8 Now, \add ≈your majesty\add*, sign the document and establish¦360079 \add ≈this law\add* so that¦360084 it \add ≈can't then be¦360104 revoked according to¦360105\add* the¦360105 law of¦360105 the¦360106 Medes and Persians.”
\v 9 \add ≈So\add* King¦360110 Dareyavesh signed¦360113 the¦360110 document¦360114 and¦360113 \add ≈it¦360125 became law¦360118\add*.
\rem /s1 Daniel in the Lions' Den
\v 10 \add ≈However although\add* Daniel knew that \add ≈that law had been passed\add*, he¦360134 \add still\add* went into his house (with the¦360135 windows in his \add ≈upper room\add* open toward Yerushalem), and he continued to kneel three times a day to pray and give thanks to God, \add just\add* as¦360128 he'd \add ≈always done\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then¦360153 those \add ≈officials\add* went¦360146 as¦360140 a group and found¦360141 Daniel¦360139 \add ≈praying¦360163 to¦360147 his god¦360166 and presenting him¦360150 with¦360151 his requests\add*.
\v 12 Then¦360181 they¦360184 approached the king and¦360185 \add ≈reminded him about¦360190 the law\add*, “Didn't you \add ≈introduce a law\add* that within thirty days, any man who makes a petition to any god¦360190 or man, except to you, \add ≈your majesty\add*, must be thrown into the lions' den?”
\p “\add ≈Yes, that's correct\add*,” answered the king. “It can't be revoked now according to¦360186 the law of¦360190 the Medes and Persians.”
\p
\v 13 Then¦360193 they¦360194 \add ≈informed\add* the¦360198 king¦360198, “Daniel who¦360210 was¦360229 exiled from¦360213 Yehudah, pays no attention to¦360227 you, \add ≈your majesty\add*, or to¦360234 the¦360234 \add ≈law¦360234 that¦360238 you introduced\add*, because he¦360212 petitions \add his god\add* three times a day¦360221.”
\p
\v 14 When¦360244 the¦360248 king¦360248 heard \add ≈that\add*, was very upset and¦360246 \add ≈for that¦360249 entire day¦360272, he¦360260 actively tried to think of a way to get Daniel¦360250 out of it\add*.
\v 15 \add ≈However, the group of men returned\add* to¦360284 the king and said, “You know, \add ≈your majesty\add*, that it's¦360283 a law of the Medes and Persians, that no rule or law that the king establishes can be¦360293 changed.”
\p
\v 16 \add ≈So\add* the king gave an order¦360305, and Daniel was brought in¦360298 and the king responded¦360305 and told¦360305 \add *him\add*, “May your god, who you serve continually, save you.” Then¦360298 Daniel was thrown \add down\add* into the lions' den.
\v 17 A stone was¦360337 brought¦360331 and¦360333 laid over the opening of the den¦360334, and the king¦360338 sealed it¦360347 with his own signet ring and with the¦360348 signet rings of his nobles so that \add ≈no one could¦360350 sneak Daniel¦360332 out\add*.
\v 18 Then the king¦360362 went to his¦360363 palace and spent the night \add ≈without eating\add*. \add ≈He¦360361 refused all\add* entertainment, and \add ≈he¦360361 couldn't sleep\add*.
\s2 Daniel's saved from the lions
\rem /s1 Daniel Saved from the Lions
\p
\v 19 Then¦360376 the king¦360376 got up at dawn, and¦360378 \add ≈hurried out\add* to¦360377 the¦360378 lions' den.
\v 20 As he¦360393 \add ≈approached\add* the den¦360396, he called out in¦360392 a worried voice, “Daniel, Daniel, servant of¦360397 the living god. Has your god, who you serve continually, been able to¦360396 save you from the lions¦360399?”
\p
\v 21 “\add ≈Long live¦360416\add* the¦360404 king¦360410,” Daniel¦360405 \add ≈called¦360408 back\add*.
\v 22 “My god sent his angel and shut the¦360436 lions' mouths, and they haven't hurt me, because \add ≈he¦360437 saw that I wasn't guilty of anything\add*. Also, \add ≈your majesty\add*, I haven't \add ≈wronged you in any way\add*.”
\p
\v 23 Then the king¦360462 was very happy, and ordered that¦360454 Daniel be pulled up out of the den. So Daniel was lifted out of the den, and \add ≈he didn't¦360449 even have¦360465 a scratch\add* on him¦360455, because¦360453 he had¦360465 trusted in his¦360445 god¦360443.
\v 24 \add Then\add* the¦360469 king¦360469 \add ≈ordered¦360474 the arrest of\add* the men who had maliciously accused Daniel¦360473, and \add ≈had them and their wives and children thrown\add* into the¦360483 lions' den¦360483—they didn't¦360487 \add even\add* reach the bottom of the den before the lions overpowered them and \add ≈tore them to¦360472 pieces\add*.
\p
\v 25 Then¦360497 King¦360497 Dareyavesh¦360497 \add !(Darius¦360497)\add* wrote to¦360507 all the¦360507 peoples, nations, and \add ≈ethnic groups right across his¦360504 kingdom\add*: “May¦360506 you all \add ≈be enjoying peace and prosperity\add*.
\v 26 I decree that¦360539 \add ≈everyone¦360543 throughout\add* my kingdom is to¦360534 \add ≈obey and respect\add* Daniel's god, because he¦360533 is the¦360538 \add ≈eternal\add*, living¦360541 God. His kingdom \add ≈will never\add* be destroyed, and his \add ≈rule will continue forever\add*.
\v 27 He delivers and rescues, and¦360578 he¦360578 does \add ≈all¦360557 kinds of miracles\add* in¦360557 the¦360559 heavens and on earth. He's¦360574 rescued Daniel¦360571 from¦360550 the \add ≈strength and power\add* of the¦360588 lions.”
\p
\v 28 \add ≈So\add* Daniel¦360600 prospered during the reign of Dareyavesh and¦360597 during the reign of¦360603 Koresh \add !(Cyrus)\add* the¦360604 Persian.
\c 7
\ms1 Daniel's visions
\mr (7:1–12:13)
\s1 Daniel's vision of four creatures
\rem /s1 Daniel's Vision of Four Beasts; Daniel's Vision of the Four Beasts; Visions of the Four Beasts; Daniel's visions; Daniel's Dream of Four Beasts
\p
\v 1 \add Previously,\add* in¦360618 Babylonian King¦360621 Belshatstsar's first year¦360618, Daniel¦360623 had seen¦360625 a dream and¦360626 visions¦360626 in his¦360627 mind while lying on¦360628 his bed¦360630. Then¦360631 he¦360633 wrote¦360633 the¦360634 dream down and \add ≈gave a summary of¦360634 it\add*.
\v 2 \add *He¦360639\add* \add ≈explained\add*:
\p In¦360643 my¦360643 vision¦360643 at¦360643 night¦360646 I¦360642 was¦360638 looking, and¦360647 \add ≈suddenly\add*, the¦360648 four¦360648 winds¦360649 were¦360651 stirring¦360651 up¦360651 the¦360652 massive ocean¦360652,
\v 3 and¦360655 four¦360655 huge¦360657 creatures¦360656, each one different¦360662 from¦360659 the¦360661 other, were¦360662 coming¦360658 up¦360658 out of \add *it\add*.\x + \xo 7:3: \xt Rev 13:1; 17:8.\x*
\li1
\v 4 The¦360685 first¦360668 \add one\add* was¦360669 like¦360669 a lion¦360669 but¦360670 had the¦360685 wings of¦360671 an¦360673 eagle¦360673. \add ≈But¦360670 as I¦360676 watched, its¦360681 wings were¦360674 torn¦360680\add* off¦360680, and¦360682 it¦360682 was¦360682 lifted¦360682 \add ≈up\add* and¦360686 made¦360690 to¦360690 stand¦360690 on¦360686 two¦360688 feet¦360688 like¦360689 a man, and¦360691 then it¦360693 was¦360693 given¦360693 a \add ≈human¦360692\add* mind¦360691.\x + \xo 7:4-6: \xt Rev 13:2.\x*
\li1
\v 5 The second¦360699 creature¦360697 resembled a bear¦360701 \add ≈with¦360708 one¦360704 arm raised¦360705\add*. \add ≈It¦360705 was¦360705 chomping on\add* three¦360706 ribs¦360707 and¦360712 it¦360714 was told¦360713 to¦360702 go and¦360706 \add ≈gorge itself\add* on \add more\add* meat¦360717.
\li1
\v 6 \add ≈Next I¦360723 saw¦360722 another¦360725 creature¦360739 that¦360721 resembled\add* a leopard¦360726, \add ≈but¦360724\add* with four wings \add ≈(like¦360726 birds¦360732' wings) attached to\add* its¦360735 back¦360735, and¦360737 with four¦360737 heads¦360738. \add ≈It¦360741 was¦360741 given¦360741 the¦360739 authority to rule¦360740 people\add*.
\li1
\v 7 The¦360748 final creature¦360751 that I¦360747 saw¦360746 in¦360748 the vision¦360748 was¦360754 dreadful¦360755 and terrifying¦360754. It¦360762 was extremely strong¦360756 with¦360748 large¦360763 iron¦360761 teeth¦360758 and it¦360764 was¦360764 devouring¦360764 \add >things¦360764\add* then trampling¦360769 and crushing¦360765 the¦360766 remainder¦360766. \add ≈Another difference¦360771 from¦360772 the¦360776 other creatures¦360776 was¦360778 that it¦360778 had ten¦360780 horns¦360779 on¦360748 its¦360767 head\add*.\x + \xo 7:7: \xt Rev 12:3; 13:1; 17:3.\x*
\p
\v 8 While I¦360784 was¦360784 considering¦360783 the¦360785 horns¦360785, \add ≈suddenly\add* another¦360788 horn¦360787, a little¦360789 one, came¦360790 up¦360790 among¦360791 \add ≈the others\add* and¦360793 three¦360793 of¦360794 the first¦360797 horns \add ≈that were¦360798 in¦360785 its way got pulled completely out\add*. \add ≈Incredibly\add*, this¦360810 \add new\add* had eyes¦360805 like¦360806 a person and¦360811 a mouth¦360811 \add ≈that had a lot of incredible¦360813 things to say¦360812\add*.\x + \xo 7:8,11: \xt Rev 13:5.\x*
\rem /s1 The revelation of God concerning the one not die
\rem /s1 Judgment before the Ancient One; The Vision of the One Who Has Been Living Forever
\q1
\v 9 I¦360816 kept looking \add in the vision\add* while thrones¦360819 were¦360820 set up and¦360821 the ancient¦360821 \add ≈one\add* took his seat¦360832. His clothing¦360824 was¦360827 as white¦360827 as snow¦360826, and¦360828 his hair¦360828 \add looked\add* like¦360830 pure¦360831 wool¦360830. His throne¦360832 was¦360833 \add ≈flaming¦360833 with\add* fire¦360836, \add and\add* its¦360837 wheels¦360837 were¦360838 burning¦360839 fire.\x + \xo 7:9: a \xt Rev 20:4; \xo b \xt Rev 1:14.\x*
\v 10 A river¦360841 of¦360842 fire¦360844 was¦360845 coming¦360846 out¦360846 and flowing¦360845 \add ≈along in front of\add* him. Thousands¦360851 and thousands¦360851 served¦360853 him¦360853, and¦360854 myriads¦360854 upon myriads¦360855 were¦360858 standing¦360858 \add ≈in front of\add* him. The¦360859 court¦360859 sat¦360860 \add in session\add* and the¦360859 documents were¦360862 opened¦360862.\x + \xo 7:10: a \xt Rev 5:11; \xo b \xt Rev 20:12.\x*
\p
\v 11 Then¦360866 I continued looking because of¦360869 the \add ≈amazing things\add* that¦360872 the¦360873 horn¦360873 was¦360874 saying¦360874. I kept looking until¦360877 that¦360878 \add final\add* creature¦360880 was killed¦360879 and¦360881 its¦360882 body¦360882 destroyed¦360881 \add ≈in¦360866\add* the fire¦360885.
\v 12 As for the¦360887 other creatures¦360888, their¦360890 \add ≈authority to rule¦360890\add* was taken¦360889 away, but¦360887 an extension¦360891 of¦360887 life¦360892 was¦360893 given¦360893 to¦360894 them¦360894 for a season and¦360887 a time¦360897.
\p
\v 13 I¦360901 was¦360901 looking in¦360902 the¦360902 visions¦360902 of¦360902 the¦360903 night¦360903, and¦360904 \add ≈suddenly\add*, \add ≈someone like¦360909 a human¦360910 male\add* was coming¦360911 with¦360902 the¦360907 clouds¦360907, and he¦360912 came¦360911 to¦360913 the¦360915 ancient¦360915 \add ≈one\add* and¦360918 was presented¦360919 to him¦360919.\x + \xo 7:13: \xt Mat 24:30; 26:64; Mrk 13:26; 14:62; Luk 21:27; Rev 1:7,13; 14:14.\x*
\v 14 He was¦360922 given¦360922 \add ≈authority to¦360921 rule\add* and \add ≈splendour\add* and a¦360933 kingdom, so that¦360935 all¦360926 the¦360927 peoples¦360927, nations¦360928, and \add ≈ethnic groups\add* will serve him¦360921. His¦360932 \add ≈authority will¦360938 never end\add*, and¦360939 his¦360939 kingdom \add ≈will¦360943 never\add* be¦360943 destroyed¦360943.\x + \xo 7:14: \xt Rev 11:15.\x*
\s1 The meaning of the visions
